      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find \\(\\cos C\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we need to determine the lengths of the sides \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\), and \\(AC\\). We are given:\n- \\(AB = 6\\)\n- \\(BC = 10\\)\n\nSince \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), triangle \\(ABC\\) is a right triangle with \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) as the legs and \\(BC\\) as the hypotenuse. We can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\(AC\\):\n\n\\[\nBC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\n10^2 = 6^2 + AC^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n100 = 36 + AC^2\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(AC^2\\):\n\n\\[\nAC^2 = 100 - 36\n\\]\n\n\\[\nAC^2 = 64\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{64} = 8\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\cos C\\). In a right triangle, the cosine of an angle is the ratio of the length of the adjacent side to the hypotenuse. For \\(\\angle C\\), the adjacent side is \\(AB\\) and the hypotenuse is \\(BC\\):\n\n\\[\n\\cos C = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{6}{10} = \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{5}}\n\\]",
