      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for \\(\\cos C\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we start by identifying the sides of the triangle. Given \\(AB = 6\\) and \\(BC = 10\\), we can determine the length of the third side, \\(AC\\), using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. Here, \\(BC\\) is the hypotenuse, so we have:\n\\[\nBC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\\[\n10^2 = 6^2 + AC^2\n\\]\n\\[\n100 = 36 + AC^2\n\\]\nSubtracting 36 from both sides, we find:\n\\[\nAC^2 = 64\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nAC = 8\n\\]\nNow, we need to find \\(\\cos C\\). In a right triangle, \\(\\cos C\\) is the ratio of the length of the adjacent side to the hypotenuse. Here, the adjacent side to \\(\\angle C\\) is \\(AB\\) and the hypotenuse is \\(BC\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\cos C = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{6}{10} = \\frac{3}{5}\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(\\cos C\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{5}}\n\\]",
